  {"id":81572,"slug":"lethocerus-americanus","sci":"Lethocerus americanus","common":"American Giant Water Bug","family":"Belostomatidae","obs":1017,"regions":["north-cascades","central-cascades","rainier","south-cascades","gorge","olympics","oregon-cascades"],"months":[4,5,6,7,8,9,10],"photos":[{"id":40396063,"ext":"jpg","by":"John Christensen","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":472738,"ext":"jpg","by":"Ken-ichi Ueda","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":85699958,"ext":"jpg","by":"znot","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":53534995,"ext":"jpeg","by":"William (Bill) Mountan","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":12987626,"ext":"jpg","by":"Jacques Larivée","lic":"CC BY-NC"}],"wiki":"Lethocerus americanus","summary":"Lethocerus americanus is a giant water bug in the family Belostomatidae, native to southern Canada and the United States (north of 35°N; other Lethocerus species are found southwards). It typically has a length around 5–6 cm (2.0–2.4 in). It was originally classified as a species in genus Belostoma."},
  {"id":360042,"slug":"campyloneura-virgula","sci":"Campyloneura virgula","common":"","family":"Miridae","obs":992,"regions":["north-cascades","oregon-cascades"],"months":[5,6,7],"photos":[{"id":42198032,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Alexis Tinker-Tsavalas","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":528267260,"ext":"jpg","by":"Christian Sørensen","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":74131407,"ext":"jpg","by":"Philippe  Garcelon","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":68661494,"ext":"jpg","by":"Elizabeth Cash","lic":"CC BY-NC"}],"wiki":"Campyloneura virgula","summary":"Campyloneura virgula is a species of bugs in Miridae family. It is the only species in the genus Campyloneura Fieber, 1861."},
  {"id":56521,"slug":"reduvius-personatus","sci":"Reduvius personatus","common":"Masked Hunter","family":"Reduviidae","obs":878,"regions":["north-cascades","central-cascades","gorge"],"months":[5,6,7,8,9],"photos":[{"id":153810479,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Tyson Ehlers","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":111034193,"ext":"jpg","by":"knuttutgut","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":59856093,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Jakob Fahr","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":146207,"ext":"jpg","by":"Valter Jacinto | Portugal","lic":"CC BY-NC-SA"},{"id":72462733,"ext":"jpg","by":"Magnefl","lic":"CC BY-SA"}],"wiki":"Reduvius personatus","summary":"Reduvius personatus or the masked hunter is an insect belonging to the assassin bug family. The name is because its nymphs camouflage themselves with dust. The masked hunter is a predator of small arthropods, including woodlice, lacewings, earwigs, bed bugs and termites. Masked hunters do not feed on human blood, but can bite humans in self-defense when mishandled."},
  {"id":261608,"slug":"neacoryphus-bicrucis","sci":"Neacoryphus bicrucis","common":"White-crossed Seed Bug","family":"Lygaeidae","obs":766,"regions":["north-cascades","central-cascades","rainier","south-cascades","gorge","olympics","oregon-cascades"],"months":[5,6,7,8,10],"photos":[{"id":12419141,"ext":"jpg","by":"Judy Gallagher","lic":"CC BY-SA"},{"id":285407716,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Mike Quinn, San Marcos","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":285408949,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Mike Quinn, San Marcos","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":7921979,"ext":"jpg","by":"an iNaturalist observer","lic":"CC0"},{"id":4672101,"ext":"jpg","by":"Mary Keim","lic":"CC BY-NC-SA"}],"wiki":"Neacoryphus bicrucis","summary":"Neacoryphus bicrucis is a species in the family Lygaeidae (\"seed bugs\"), in the order Hemiptera (\"true bugs, cicadas, hoppers, aphids and allies\"). The species is known generally as the \"whitecrossed seed bug\" or \"ragwort seed bug\". The distribution range of Neacoryphus bicrucis includes Central America, North America, Oceania, and South America."},
  {"id":366934,"slug":"limnoporus-notabilis","sci":"Limnoporus notabilis","common":"","family":"Gerridae","obs":599,"regions":["north-cascades","central-cascades","rainier","south-cascades","oregon-cascades"],"months":[4,5,6,7,8,9],"photos":[{"id":96397856,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Pam Piombino","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Water_Strider_on_Lily_Lake_%2815058406212%29.jpg/1280px-Water_Strider_on_Lily_Lake_%2815058406212%29.jpg","by":"Katja Schulz from Washington, D. C., USA","lic":"CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Water_Strider_on_Lily_Lake_(15058406212).jpg"},{"id":558747569,"ext":"jpg","by":"Colton Veltkamp","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":494806367,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Liam Ragan","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":417422988,"ext":"jpeg","by":"paintedbunting123","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":726663365,"ext":"jpg","by":"Remington Tengs Jackson","lic":"CC BY-NC"}],"wiki":"Limnoporus notabilis","summary":"Limnoporus notabilis is a species of water strider in the family Gerridae. It is found in North America."},
  {"id":141725,"slug":"nezara-viridula","sci":"Nezara viridula","common":"Southern Green Stink Bug","family":"Pentatomidae","obs":579,"regions":["central-cascades"],"months":[7,8,9,10],"photos":[{"id":237377959,"ext":"jpg","by":"Davide G. Borin","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":567739812,"ext":"jpg","by":"Gábor Keresztes","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":66906867,"ext":"jpg","by":"Karol Ox","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":350223798,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Martin Klippel","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":99827077,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Hayath Mohammed","lic":"CC BY-SA"}],"wiki":"Nezara viridula","summary":"Nezara viridula, commonly known as the southern green stink bug (USA), Southern green shield bug (UK) or green vegetable bug (Australia and New Zealand), is a plant-feeding stink bug. Although believed to have originated in Ethiopia, it can now be found around the world. Because of its preference for certain species of legumes, such as beans and soybeans, it is an economically important pest on such crops."},
  {"id":505749,"slug":"arocatus-melanocephalus","sci":"Arocatus melanocephalus","common":"Elm Seed Bug","family":"Lygaeidae","obs":566,"regions":["central-cascades"],"months":[3,4,5,6,7,8,9,10,11],"photos":[{"id":65291697,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Alexis Tinker-Tsavalas","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":65397698,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Alexis Tinker-Tsavalas","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":80951834,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Alexis Tinker-Tsavalas","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":80882459,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Sam","lic":"CC BY-NC"}],"wiki":"Arocatus melanocephalus","summary":"Arocatus melanocephalus, the elm seed bug, is a true bug in the family Lygaeidae. The species was initially described by Johan Christian Fabricius in 1798, and Maximilian Spinola designated it to be the type species of the genus Arocatus in 1837. This bug is native to Europe but has been introduced to North America."},
  {"id":82832,"slug":"cosmopepla-lintneriana","sci":"Cosmopepla lintneriana","common":"Twice-stabbed Stink Bug","family":"Pentatomidae","obs":534,"regions":["north-cascades","south-cascades","olympics","oregon-cascades"],"months":[5,6,7,8,9],"photos":[{"id":55448883,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Reiner Jakubowski","lic":"CC BY-SA"},{"id":11587602,"ext":"jpg","by":"Joe Bartok","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":1921197,"ext":"jpg","by":"Scott King","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":49334654,"ext":"jpeg","by":"lynnelamoureux","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":6272868,"ext":"jpg","by":"Fyn Kynd","lic":"CC BY-SA"}],"wiki":"Cosmopepla lintneriana","summary":"Cosmopepla lintneriana, the twice-stabbed stink bug, locally called the wee harlequin bug, is a species of insect in the Pentatomidae family. Cosmopepla lintneriana was first described in 1798 by Johan Christian Fabricius as Cimex carnifex and then again in 1865 by Thomas as Cosmopepla bimaculata."},
  {"id":133757,"slug":"ranatra-fusca","sci":"Ranatra fusca","common":"Brown Waterscorpion","family":"Nepidae","obs":499,"regions":["north-cascades","south-cascades","gorge","olympics","oregon-cascades"],"months":[4,5,6,7,8,9],"photos":[{"id":84570630,"ext":"jpg","by":"Jimmy Dee","lic":"CC BY-SA"},{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Brown_water_scorpion.jpg/1280px-Brown_water_scorpion.jpg","by":"Greg Hume","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brown_water_scorpion.jpg"}],"wiki":"Ranatra fusca","summary":"Ranatra fusca is a water stick-insect in the family Nepidae, native to North America. It is known by the common name brown water scorpion."},
